WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.267
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy-nominated actor

00:00:03.267 --> 00:00:06.200
you know from his work in "Oz,"
"Wet Hot American Summer"

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.934
and his Detective Elliot Stabler

00:00:07.934 --> 00:00:09.434
in "Law &amp; Order:
Organized Crime."

00:00:09.434 --> 00:00:12.334
New episodes air Thursday nights
at 10:00 right here on NBC.

00:00:12.334 --> 00:00:13.534
Let's take a look.
[ Cheers ]

00:00:13.534 --> 00:00:14.968
-You met with Octavio earlier
today?

00:00:14.968 --> 00:00:16.200
-I did.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.801
All those failed investigations,
thing of the past.

00:00:18.801 --> 00:00:21.267
We got Octavio.
We're gonna get Murphy.

00:00:21.267 --> 00:00:23.267
-No, we're not.
-No, Octavio's gonna give me

00:00:23.267 --> 00:00:25.701
a statement that places Murphy
as the shooter.

00:00:25.701 --> 00:00:27.267
-Octavio is dead.

00:00:27.267 --> 00:00:29.234
[ Ominous music plays ]

00:00:29.234 --> 00:00:30.934
-What?
-They couldn't get ahold of you.

00:00:30.934 --> 00:00:32.167
The warden called me.

00:00:32.167 --> 00:00:34.434
♪♪

00:00:34.434 --> 00:00:36.834
Octavio was murdered tonight.

00:00:36.834 --> 00:00:39.667
They found his body
outside the showers.

00:00:39.667 --> 00:00:40.701
-Please welcome back
to the show,

00:00:40.701 --> 00:00:42.501
Christopher Meloni, everybody!

00:00:42.501 --> 00:00:44.534
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:44.534 --> 00:00:54.534
♪♪

00:00:54.534 --> 00:00:58.400
♪♪

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.167
Welcome back to the show!

00:01:00.167 --> 00:01:01.200
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.267
-Thank you!

00:01:03.267 --> 00:01:08.167
-You pulled something off that I
think all dads would aspire to.

00:01:08.167 --> 00:01:09.534
It was your wife's birthday,
correct?

00:01:09.534 --> 00:01:11.467
-Yes.
-And you went out clubbing

00:01:11.467 --> 00:01:13.367
with your wife and...
-Yeah.

00:01:13.367 --> 00:01:15.734
-...your children came.
Your children were willing

00:01:15.734 --> 00:01:17.567
to come to the club
with their parents.

00:01:17.567 --> 00:01:19.300
-My daughter was the instigator.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:20.901
-It was her idea?
-Yes. Yeah.

00:01:20.901 --> 00:01:22.234
-Wow.
-She's got her finger

00:01:22.234 --> 00:01:25.434
on the pulse, and I pretend that
I have the same finger.

00:01:25.434 --> 00:01:27.868
-I mean, she has to then
have an expectation

00:01:27.868 --> 00:01:30.200
that you're not gonna kill her
with embarrassment at the club.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:31.934
-Yes, that's true.
-Yeah.

00:01:31.934 --> 00:01:33.767
Did you behave well,
do you think?

00:01:33.767 --> 00:01:35.067
[ Laughter ]
I mean, this is --

00:01:35.067 --> 00:01:36.801
There's some
photographing of it.

00:01:36.801 --> 00:01:37.868
-There's a lot --

00:01:37.868 --> 00:01:39.400
[ Laughter ]
-I mean, I will say --

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:40.968
-Now, do you know what
that is from, though?

00:01:40.968 --> 00:01:43.367
-What's that?
-So, my daughter and I,

00:01:43.367 --> 00:01:44.934
because it was in the middle
of the pandemic,

00:01:44.934 --> 00:01:48.033
we couldn't celebrate
my 60th and her 20th.

00:01:48.033 --> 00:01:49.734
-Okay. Got it.
-So they're very close together,

00:01:49.734 --> 00:01:52.334
our birthdays -- she's
March 23rd, I'm April 2nd.

00:01:52.334 --> 00:01:56.534
So we did 21/61.
-Oh, very nice.

00:01:56.534 --> 00:01:58.567
-Yeah.
I did an Evel Knievel look.

00:01:58.567 --> 00:02:01.701
-I will say, if you didn't know
your dad was gonna be

00:02:01.701 --> 00:02:03.467
at the club, and you saw this,
it would be a nightmare.

00:02:03.467 --> 00:02:04.701
-Let's see.
-I would say that

00:02:04.701 --> 00:02:06.667
would not be...
[ Laughter ]

00:02:06.667 --> 00:02:07.901
-Okay.
-If you all of a sudden

00:02:07.901 --> 00:02:09.334
were like, if someone came over,
was like,

00:02:09.334 --> 00:02:11.667
"I think your dad's here,"
it would be pretty devastating.

00:02:11.667 --> 00:02:13.300
[ Laughter ]

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:16.501
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:16.501 --> 00:02:19.367
So --
-I always get this.

00:02:19.367 --> 00:02:21.167
"Your dad looks really angry,"
and all I have to do

00:02:21.167 --> 00:02:22.734
is pull one of these.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:22.734 --> 00:02:25.067
"Come on in!
We got pancakes. Relax."

00:02:25.067 --> 00:02:27.968
-That's true.
I imagine, based on which

00:02:27.968 --> 00:02:29.634
part of your work
people have seen,

00:02:29.634 --> 00:02:30.934
they might think
you're angry all the time.

00:02:30.934 --> 00:02:33.701
But again, you have a whole
comedy side that

00:02:33.701 --> 00:02:34.934
people could be familiar
with that, too.

00:02:34.934 --> 00:02:36.334
-Yeah, and I kind of
straddled that with

00:02:36.334 --> 00:02:37.667
"Wet Hot American Summer,"
little crazy, little angry...

00:02:37.667 --> 00:02:39.300
-I feel like if the kids
have seen that,

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:40.501
they kind of know
you're in on the joke.

00:02:40.501 --> 00:02:41.767
That's a helpful thing.
-There you go.

00:02:41.767 --> 00:02:45.067
-This is third season of
"Organized Crime," but obviously

00:02:45.067 --> 00:02:47.067
there's a character
you've been with forever.

00:02:47.067 --> 00:02:50.367
It must be nice to play someone
like Elliot Stabler that you,

00:02:50.367 --> 00:02:52.467
at this point, must know
better than any role

00:02:52.467 --> 00:02:54.434
you could ever hope to take on.
-Yeah.

00:02:54.434 --> 00:02:55.567
You know, it's one of these
things that

00:02:55.567 --> 00:02:57.400
you know him better
than anybody.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:00.767
You know, it's -- yeah.
So, you try and, uh -- yeah.

00:03:00.767 --> 00:03:02.033
[ Laughter ]

00:03:02.033 --> 00:03:05.033
I really do know him.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:05.033 --> 00:03:06.968
-You -- one of the cool things
about it is --

00:03:06.968 --> 00:03:08.200
Oh, by the way,
I forgot to show this.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:10.968
This is after the club?
-No, that's pre-clubbing.

00:03:10.968 --> 00:03:12.100
[ Laughter ]
-Pre-club?

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:13.968
-Yeah.
-Pre-clubbing.

00:03:13.968 --> 00:03:16.767
-Yeah, that's how -- I entered
the club like that.

00:03:16.767 --> 00:03:18.033
[ Laughter ]

00:03:18.033 --> 00:03:19.467
-And do you know
how I was greeted

00:03:19.467 --> 00:03:21.601
by the woman at the door?

00:03:21.601 --> 00:03:24.200
Like this.
She went...

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:25.467
-Oh, wow.

00:03:25.467 --> 00:03:27.701
-And I went like this.

00:03:27.701 --> 00:03:29.334
[ Laughter ]

00:03:29.334 --> 00:03:31.367
-And I entered,
and I partied, and I danced.

00:03:31.367 --> 00:03:32.934
-Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:32.934 --> 00:03:36.000
-I'm assuming --
-Thank you.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.834
Probably -- They probably didn't
card you, I'm gonna guess.

00:03:38.834 --> 00:03:40.367
-No, no.
-Yeah.

00:03:40.367 --> 00:03:42.300
-Made me angry,
but I let it pass.

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:43.567
[ Laughter ]

00:03:43.567 --> 00:03:46.634
-This is so -- you shoot
on the streets of New York.

00:03:46.634 --> 00:03:48.434
You've obviously worked with
so many actors over the years.

00:03:48.434 --> 00:03:50.133
I mean, that's one of
the great things about

00:03:50.133 --> 00:03:51.968
the "Law &amp; Order" franchise --
one, you shoot on the streets.

00:03:51.968 --> 00:03:53.934
Two, you shoot with every
great New York actor.

00:03:53.934 --> 00:03:55.667
Over the years,
almost everybody.

00:03:55.667 --> 00:03:58.634
And this is the great Ann Dowd.
And you've, uh --

00:03:58.634 --> 00:04:01.000
When was the first time
you guys crossed paths?

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.767
-She did an episode of "SVU."
-Back in the day?

00:04:03.767 --> 00:04:06.901
-Back, back, way back,
like 20 years ago.

00:04:06.901 --> 00:04:09.934
And I was embarrassed
that I didn't know who she was.

00:04:09.934 --> 00:04:12.033
Because when you act
with Ann Dowd,

00:04:12.033 --> 00:04:16.234
you are just blown back 20 feet
because she's such a powerful,

00:04:16.234 --> 00:04:17.968
creative actor.

00:04:17.968 --> 00:04:20.200
And so any way, I was like,
"Oh, my God.

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:22.267
Who the hell is this?
I've just discovered you,"

00:04:22.267 --> 00:04:24.534
and she's very famous.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:24.534 --> 00:04:27.434
-Yeah.
-So, anyway, I worked with her

00:04:27.434 --> 00:04:29.400
on "Handmaid's Tale."
-Yep, you were great in that.

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:33.234
Both of you.
-And she's --

00:04:33.234 --> 00:04:35.200
I don't know her name,
'cause I watched it,

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:37.100
but it was a thousand years ago
on that one.

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:39.167
But her character
on "Handmaid's Tale"...

00:04:39.167 --> 00:04:41.434
-Lydia. Aunt Lydia.
-...is so evil.

00:04:41.434 --> 00:04:43.501
-Yeah, she's a bad --
-And she couldn't be,

00:04:43.501 --> 00:04:45.801
as a person, obviously,
more lovely.

00:04:45.801 --> 00:04:48.467
But anyway, I bumped into her
on the street.

00:04:48.467 --> 00:04:50.400
And we have this big love fest.

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:52.501
And we're catching up

00:04:52.501 --> 00:04:54.200
because we don't get
to see each other too much.

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:56.133
And this woman comes up to us.

00:04:56.133 --> 00:04:58.934
She goes, "Oh, my God.
I love you so much."

00:04:58.934 --> 00:05:00.434
And I turned around, and I said
"Thank you."

00:05:00.434 --> 00:05:04.334
She goes, "Not you."
[ Laughter ]

00:05:04.334 --> 00:05:06.434
"Her."
And I laughed at myself.

00:05:06.434 --> 00:05:08.167
I was like,
"Me, too! Me, too!

00:05:08.167 --> 00:05:11.300
I love her more than me, too!"
[ Laughter ]

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:14.000
-That's why I never stand with
another famous person in public.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:15.133
I can't take that risk.

00:05:15.133 --> 00:05:16.300
-That's why you always
run away from me.

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:17.400
-Yeah, when I see you
on the street, I'm like,

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:19.167
"I don't want to risk it!"
[ Laughter ]

00:05:19.167 --> 00:05:21.200
"A lot of Meloni fans
out on these streets."

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:24.467
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:24.467 --> 00:05:27.767
Now, I know -- I feel like these
are "Law &amp; Order" fans,

00:05:27.767 --> 00:05:30.033
but I was a fan of your early
commercial work.

00:05:30.033 --> 00:05:32.334
[ Laughter ]

00:05:32.334 --> 00:05:34.501
So, you did --
This is your early days.

00:05:34.501 --> 00:05:36.634
-Yeah. This is --
This is what got me out of

00:05:36.634 --> 00:05:39.033
the bars, and not from drinking
but from being a bouncer

00:05:39.033 --> 00:05:40.400
and a bartender and all that.
-Gotcha.

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:44.067
-You did both a Burger King
and a McDonald's, correct?

00:05:44.067 --> 00:05:45.701
-Oh, man, I did like
50 commercials.

00:05:45.701 --> 00:05:47.300
-Gotcha.
And those were --

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:50.968
I mean, again, all jokes aside,
that was real money

00:05:50.968 --> 00:05:52.701
to do a commercial,
and the more it played,

00:05:52.701 --> 00:05:54.200
the better off you do.
-Residuals.

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:55.734
-Yeah.
-Sure. I mean --

00:05:55.734 --> 00:05:58.667
I would go to my --
You know, guys from New York,

00:05:58.667 --> 00:06:02.267
you know, you have those little
mailboxes like that,

00:06:02.267 --> 00:06:03.868
and you'd stick that key in.

00:06:03.868 --> 00:06:06.367
If there's a dull thud,
you know it's stuffed.

00:06:06.367 --> 00:06:07.934
[ Laughter ]

00:06:07.934 --> 00:06:11.567
And I didn't have a lot of bills
because I had -- I had no TV.

00:06:11.567 --> 00:06:12.801
I had nothing.

00:06:12.801 --> 00:06:15.234
And it would go thud,
and I'd open it, and --

00:06:15.234 --> 00:06:18.868
pbt! -- springing out would be
all these residual checks.

00:06:18.868 --> 00:06:21.334
-And sometimes they --
you actually can't believe

00:06:21.334 --> 00:06:23.167
for the work of the envelope
and the stamp

00:06:23.167 --> 00:06:25.767
how little those residual --
-36 cents.

00:06:25.767 --> 00:06:27.234
[ Laughter ]
-It all adds up.

00:06:27.234 --> 00:06:29.367
-Or $2,500.
-Well, there you go.

00:06:29.367 --> 00:06:31.467
-It's like a lottery.
It's like rolling the dice.

00:06:31.467 --> 00:06:34.467
You open up the envelope,
"What's it gonna be?"

00:06:34.467 --> 00:06:37.467
-I want to show a clip from,
I think, Burger King is first.

00:06:37.467 --> 00:06:39.000
Let's take a look at this.
-I don't even remember this.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:40.868
-Oh, this is very good.
Let's take a look.

00:06:40.868 --> 00:06:44.267
-Yumbo, the hot ham and cheese
sandwich now at Burger King,

00:06:44.267 --> 00:06:46.000
and, one day,
in your dictionary.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:47.667
-Yum.

00:06:47.667 --> 00:06:52.567
-I mean...
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:54.467 --> 00:06:55.734
-Great [bleep]
-Yeah.

00:06:55.734 --> 00:06:57.400
[ Laughter ]

00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:00.767
-That's -- That's talent,
number one.

00:07:00.767 --> 00:07:04.634
But number two, and this is --
Take this story home with you.

00:07:04.634 --> 00:07:07.501
It'll be great to tell people
over dinner or something.

00:07:07.501 --> 00:07:12.634
The behind-the-scenes is,
I go, and I get the job,

00:07:12.634 --> 00:07:16.300
and the producer
looks at my hands and he goes,

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:20.801
"Your nails and your cuticles,
like, with this face."

00:07:20.801 --> 00:07:25.167
He goes, "Get a manicure."
I go, "Okay."

00:07:25.167 --> 00:07:27.634
They called a manicure place,

00:07:27.634 --> 00:07:30.000
opened up early 'cause we were
shooting at 8:00 a.m.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:31.801
They had a manicure place
open at 7:00 a.m.

00:07:31.801 --> 00:07:33.767
There are not too many
manicure places open at 7:00.

00:07:33.767 --> 00:07:36.434
I'd never had a manicure.
They do my nails,

00:07:36.434 --> 00:07:40.234
and the lady says,
"Do you want polish?"

00:07:40.234 --> 00:07:42.467
And, you know, we're New York.

00:07:42.467 --> 00:07:44.834
I'd never had a manicure.
And you see a lot of them guys,

00:07:44.834 --> 00:07:47.834
you know, with the shiny --
-Yeah, they look nice.

00:07:47.834 --> 00:07:49.734
-You know, they look nice.
-Tough guys, nice fingers.

00:07:49.734 --> 00:07:53.534
-Your nails look nice.
So I got a polish.

00:07:53.534 --> 00:07:56.467
And the producer hit the roof.

00:07:56.467 --> 00:07:57.968
[ Laughter ]

00:07:57.968 --> 00:08:00.400
He went nuts on me.
He was yelling at me.

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:01.501
There you go.
-So that's --

00:08:01.501 --> 00:08:03.434
And, again,
not a lot of people --

00:08:03.434 --> 00:08:05.400
People forget
about the burger wars,

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:08.000
but in this era, it was very --
you had to be quite an actor

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:09.501
to go from
a Burger King commercial

00:08:09.501 --> 00:08:11.601
to a McDonald's commercial.
-Oh, yeah.

00:08:11.601 --> 00:08:14.167
-Very few could do both.
-No. Thank you.

00:08:14.167 --> 00:08:15.901
-I know we are talking about
comedy and drama,

00:08:15.901 --> 00:08:17.934
but to do a Burger King
and then a McDonald's...

00:08:17.934 --> 00:08:20.000
-Yes, truly.
-And the McDonald's one,

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:22.100
I want to show it twice.
But the first ti--

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:24.968
I want to show it first and hear
what your memory of it was.

00:08:24.968 --> 00:08:26.400
Let's take a look.
-Therefore, if you want to

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:28.300
go out with me, we will not
be going to any restaurant

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:33.400
which refers to itself
as a bistro, a casa, a maison.

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:35.501
We will be going to McDonald's.

00:08:35.501 --> 00:08:38.167
[ Laughter ]

00:08:38.167 --> 00:08:41.200
-There you are, on a date,
insisting to a woman

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:43.501
that you will
only be going to McDonald's.

00:08:43.501 --> 00:08:45.133
[ Laughter ]

00:08:45.133 --> 00:08:47.434
-Laying down the law.
-Yeah.

00:08:47.434 --> 00:08:49.100
-Yeah.
Women like strong men.

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:50.434
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:50.434 --> 00:08:53.234
I was gonna say that,
if you watch it, it also feels

00:08:53.234 --> 00:08:56.300
like one of those Lifetime
movies where a woman is,

00:08:56.300 --> 00:08:57.901
for the first time realizing,
like,

00:08:57.901 --> 00:09:00.467
"Oh, this guy's a full lunatic."

00:09:00.467 --> 00:09:03.400
[ Laughter ]

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:05.634
-Mike Lindell's first date.
-Yeah.

00:09:05.634 --> 00:09:08.567
[ Laughter ]

00:09:08.567 --> 00:09:10.567
-It was all pillow-based.

00:09:10.567 --> 00:09:12.534
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:12.534 --> 00:09:14.267
Man, oh, man, what a pleasure
to have you here.

00:09:14.267 --> 00:09:16.200
Congratulations again
on season 3.

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:17.501
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:17.501 --> 00:09:20.100
Christopher Meloni!
"Law &amp; Order: Organized Crime"

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:22.300
airs Thursday nights
at 10:00 here on NBC.

00:09:22.300 --> 00:09:23.968
We'll be right back
with D'Arcy Carden.

